 \label{SLPvUH}
Let $L_1,\ldots, L_r$ be distinct linear forms on $\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}^n$, and let $Z$ be the set
of points in $\ensuremath{\mathbb{P}}^n$ dual to the hyperplanes defined by the $L_i$. Fix integers $d\geq m>1$.
Then the following are equivalent:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $Z$ has an unexpected hypersurface of degree $d$ with a general point $P$ of multiplicity~$m$;
\item[(b)] $R/(L_1^{d},\ldots, L_r^{d})$ fails SLP in degree $i=m-1$ with range $k=d-m+1$.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{equation} \label{LES}
